an uneasy mood has settled over Baghdad  scarred by decades of intermittent war  people in Iraq's capital know the eerie  suspense that precedes one in coffee  shops old and young alike worried about  being caught in the middle as the u.s.  and Iran threatened further escalation  we don't want the battle to come to our  country they want to start a war they  want to settle accounts but we're the  ones who lose young men lose money lose  infrastructure we won't benefit from  this people here are still in shock over  the u.s. strike that assassinated  Iranian general Qasem Soleimani near  Baghdad Airport growing threats of  retaliation from Iran and its allies in  Iraq adding insult to injury  President Donald Trump threatened on  Sunday to impose far-reaching sanctions  on Iraq if the Iraqi government  responded by expelling US troops for  wasan such rhetoric brings back painful  memories from the Saddam era when the UN  Security Council imposed a crippling  trade embargo nakisha Bob for us as  young people we've lived through the  sanctions during our childhood we don't  want to face the same thing again we  want to build the country instead the  country now risks sliding backwards the  unfolding crisis between the u.s. and  Iran has shifted attention away from  Iraq's month-long protest movement  demanding far-reaching political reform  and an end to Iranian interference in  Iraq since the manis assassination  demonstrators have been attacked at  least twice by pro-iranian crowds their  worst fear however is losing their cause  as the island mujeeb people people are  afraid they are afraid of the strikes  that happened and they fear that  something will happen inside Tahrir to  end these demonstrations demonstrators  are divided over the Parliament's  non-binding decision on Sunday to expel  foreign troops some support the motion  calling the US strike a violation of  Iraqi sovereignty we want Iraq to be an  independent country a country that  thinks and decides and makes its own  decisions without any intervention by  outside forces while others see the  presence of American troops as  necessary counterbalance to Iranian  influence I am against the Parliament's  decision to expel the troops give me an  alternative for the American troops who  will protect me an alternative to  protect these people they are all  parties in militias either I have a  party to protect me or I will die  Kurdish and Sunni parties boycotted  Sunday's Parliament vote carried by the  main Shia bloc's it's a sign that Iraq's  leadership is once again split along  sectarian lines rather than United by  national interest as divisions within  Iraq deepen over how the government  should respond to the escalating crisis  between the u.s. and Iran many Iraqis  are worried that they will once again be  dragged into a war they did not choose  their demands for a better future are  now overshadowed by fear and not to  distant memories of conflict and  hardship
 

